For a fixed $A_i$ (the top mochi), we want to find the number of $A_j$ (bottom mochi) such that $A_j \ge 2 A_i$.
Since $A$ is sorted, the values satisfying $A_j \ge 2 A_i$ will form a suffix of the array.
Specifically, if we find the smallest index $k$ such that $A_k \ge 2 A_i$, then all indices $j \ge k$ satisfy the condition.
The number of such $j$'s is $N - k + 1$.
If no such $k$ exists (i.e., all $A_j < 2 A_i$), the count is 0.

We can iterate $i$ from $1$ to $N$. For each $i$, we find the smallest $k$ such that $A_k \ge 2 A_i$.
Since $A_i$ is increasing as $i$ increases, the threshold $2 A_i$ is also increasing. Therefore, the index $k$ will also be non-decreasing.
This suggests a two-pointer approach.
Let $j$ be the pointer for the bottom mochi. Initially, we can set $j=1$.
